// A fused multiply-add whose multiplier AND addend are both literals
// (t_a1f43b12).
//
// The Mul matcher stores whichever operand was not the varying in a field
// called `multiplierUniformId`, and the MAD emitter read that as a
// uniform: it wrote a zero placeholder for the host to patch, and nothing
// ever patched it.  The shader compiled, exit 0, no diagnostic, and every
// pixel came out as the addend:
//
//     ours       MOVH H0, f[TEX0]; MOVR R1, {0,0,0,0}; FENCBR;
//                MADR R0, H0, R1, {0.5,0.5,0.5,0.5}     -> v * 0 + 0.5
//     reference  MOVR R0, f[TEX0];
//                MADR R0, R0, {0.5,0,0,0}.x, {0.5,0,0,0}.x
//
// Found by the rig's VP instrument rows on their first boot: ours painted
// one level where the reference painted sixty-four.
//
// Two rules are visible in the reference's shape.  An instruction carries
// a single 16-byte const block, so when the two literals' distinct values
// fit in four lanes there is no preload and no fence - both operands read
// that one block with their own swizzles.  And the varying is moved at
// FULL precision; the MOVH here rounded it to half before a float
// multiply, which no part of the source asked for.
void main(float4 v : TEXCOORD0, out float4 color : COLOR)
{
    color = v * float4(0.5f, 0.5f, 0.5f, 0.5f)
              + float4(0.5f, 0.5f, 0.5f, 0.5f);
}
